azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

I thought I had replied to this, but seems like Google Groups lost my
post.  Dagnabbit!

> [1]: Characters with very low dexterity should have intrinsic fumbling.
> --Grunt

I like this idea (although it's drastic enough that it should be a
fairly low dexterity threshold, maybe 5).  I especially like
generalizing the concept to cover all the stats:
Low strength: weapons difficult to hold, so swinging in combat
sometimes takes an extra move if weapon is two-handed.
Low constitution: many consecutive walk moves cause player to be slowed
for a while as he catches his breath.
Low wisdom: some items that produce obvious effects are randomly not
conclusively ID'd when used (player gets the "What do you want to call
it?" prompt instead).
Low charisma: sometimes peaceful monsters become hostile (not monsters
that accept money from the player though: shopkeepers, priests or the
Oracle).  This is potentially dangerous when demon summoning at altars.

> [2]: Vorpal Blade against jabberwocks should produce the yafm: "Thou
> hast slain the Jabberwock!" as opposed to the normal death message.
> --Grunt

Someone else already suggested my idea of randomly sometimes saying "Oh
frabjous day!" or "Callooh!  Callay!" after the normal message.  The
YANI message, however, is a bit more obscure: I had to check the poem
to make sure it was appropriate.  It's not one of the signature lines
of Jabberwocky.

> [3]: If you grease your gauntlets, slippery finger effects should take
> place. Further, greased weapons should slip from your hands (probably
> by making your hands greasy). --Grunt

Logical, although greasing takes a little while to wear off.  Also, I
believe the greased parts of weapons are the parts that hit monsters,
not the "handle."

> [4]: If a soldier or any variation thereof (lieutenant, sergeant,
> captain) turns to flee, any neighboring soldiers should attack him/her
> while shouting "Deserter!". --aristotle73

I don't like this idea (makes soliders easier and I consider that,
regardless of what their help text suggests, Nethack soldiers are
probably hired mercenaries), but I love the idea of making soliders
more dangerous through cooperative behavior.  I had worked up a fairly
major idea around this in the lost version of my post.  Trying to
reconstruct:
If soldiers are anywhere near (within five spaces at least) of an
officer-class solider, then they should show increased intelligence
regarding how they handle the player.  They should refuse to chase the
player into a narrow passage where they can be picked off one by one,
they should attempt to block the player from getting to those places,
they should place a greater priority in surrounding the player, if the
player gets away their priority should be to guard the downstairs, they
should spread out and not line up single-file to become easy fodder for
wands, and they should flee if they see the player has a very dangerous
weapon (especially cockatrice corpse).

> [5]: re: ideas[4]; soldiers should not flee simply because their HP
> gets low. --workimer

I like the idea that soliders are mercenaries more than that they have
a patriotic motive ("For the glory of Yendor!").


> [6]: Pets should be much less likely than other monsters to "back away
> skeptically" when you try to #untrap them, and peaceful monsters less
> likely than hostile ones. --PsyMar

Logical, but not really compelling.

> [7]: The Muumuu: Hawaiian Body armour, can be worn by 'large'
> creatures, and falls off huge ones without damage on polymorphing.
> --SpeedEvil

Oy, aren't shirts enough?

> [8]: Dipping fruit into a potion of booze should make foo-foo drinks;
> ie. a banana + booze = a banana daiquiri.  etc. --workimer

Too many items added to the game in service of a fairly minor joke.

> [9]: Monsters that cast spells should have, say 50% change of carrying
> the spellbooks for the spells they know. --ijon

Monster spells are usually innate abilities.  This may make spellbooks,
a very useful class of item, a bit too common anyway.

> [10]: Monsters should have a WIS stat for judging whether to attack you
> or run away from you (or if they're your pet, other monsters) --PsyMar

Monsters having stats are not a bad idea, but it'd have to be thought
out very carefully.  A lot of work.

> [11]: A wand of "order" or "rearranging" or somesuch. Zap it in a
> direction and the monsters within x squares will rearrange, altering
> the order in which they're lined up --offipso

Not really all that interesting a behavior.  Kind of an incomplete
idea, too.

> [12]: Potion of ipecac.  Stuns you for a few turns and reduces your
> hunger status to just above Weak, but cures (or has a chance of curing)
> food poisoning. --cjeris

Fairly special-purpose, not that interesting.  (Of course, I'd say the
same thing about potions of oil and acid.)

> [13]: Cursed wands should have some ill effects beside exploding
> occasionally. ("Flames fly from the wand.  The flames scorch your
> boots!  Your boots smoulder!") --Grunt

Interesting idea, needs effects for many wands though.  (I also think
rusty and otherwise-damaged wands should have subtly different
effects.)

> [14]: Fireproof candles should not be able to be lit. --Grunt

Are such things in the game?

> [15]: Dip things into a potion of gain energy for the same effects as a
> scroll of charging --PsyMar

I like this, although the effect should be less than a scroll of
charging and not work on wands of wishing, of course.  I especially
like anything that makes charging rings a little easier.

> [16]: (15 continued)  The player should get a message like "You dip the
> kitten corpse into the potion of gain energy.  The kitten comes to
> life!  It wields a top hat and cane!  You hear it sing show tunes!  You
> feel like dancing!  You resist!" --aristotle73

Dear god no.

> [17]: Candy bars become a different candy based on the holiday.
> Christmas = Candy cane; easter = marshmallow chicks; Valentine's day =
> candy heart or box of chocolates; etc. --aristotle73

An... interesting idea.  But not really compelling.

> [18]: Polymorphing a crocodile corpse, instead of creating fireproof
